You are absolutely right about the Nazgul stuff; they are too easy.
Just like the balrog. When I first met a Nazgul I was really scared
(I've read Lord of the rings a thousand times) and first I thought I
should run. But after a brief thinking I decided to take the challenge
and fight the nasty. I killed it with only a few blows from my Excalibur.
I was a bit - not, make that _a lot_ - depressed. 
 
You probably haven't read Tolkien very closely since you suggest _any_
metallic weapon to be broken upon contact with the Nazgul. In LOTR,
weapons made in Numenor (or other ancient weapons made by great elven-
smiths or dwarves) are effective against a Nazgul, even though they
_may_ break, too. So, elven weapons should work against the Nazgul 
in Nethack too, and _Sting_ would be very vital when fighting a
ringwraith (the weapon Sting, not the musician). 
All the other suggestions you made were pretty nice and I'd be
pleased if they were added to NH 3.1. 
 
PS. In LOTR, the word Elbereth is a great weapon against Nazguls.
I don't think this should be the case in Nethack unless the gods
were very pleased of the player's progress so far. In LOTR, not
just anyone could call for Elbereth and make the horror go away..
 
PPS. My suggestions about Balrog;
 1. He should be able to hit with his bullwhip from three square's
    distance, since the Balrog is a *large* beast. 
 2. The bullwhip should entangle the player and begin to strangle
    him (just like the amulet). 
 3. Anyone within five squares of the Balrog should get fire damage
    just like anyone hearing the Nazgul's shriek.
 4. Should the Balrog be slain, his bullwhip couldn't be used by
    anyone (see 1).
 5. Balrog should regenerate VERY fast, but not if slain (being
    a demon it would leave no corpse).
 6. Balrog, just like Nazgul, should inflict fear to anyone he
    gazes. If the player doesn't resist, he goes into panic or 
    dies (if fails the saving throw completely). The panic should
    last for 5-10 rounds and would be somewhat similar to stunning.
 
 
And for people who think we're making these two creatures too
powerful: no, we're just making them the way they are in _the_
Book. Read for yourself.
